<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Riel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1844–1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Riel was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> leader of the Red River settlement. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s Rupert's Land passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1869 without consulting its people, he formed a provisional government whose negotiations led to the creation of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> in 1870. Exiled, he returned in 1884 to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, and after its defeat he was tried for treason and hanged in 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-riel.jpg" alt="Louis Riel" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer: I. Bennetto &amp;amp; Co. (Israel Bennetto, 1860-1946[2])(possibly) Engraver: Octave-Henri Julien (1852-1908) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Riel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
